  "result": "**Short answer:** you don't need PPO for the makerspace assistant, and I won't derive it here. Deriving it needs policy gradients and probability ratios, and you haven't met those yet.\n\n**Why this works**\n\nPicture RL as a retry loop. The model attempts a task, such as booking a laser cutter. A reward says whether the booking came out right, and training nudges the model toward the attempts that scored well. PPO is a way to keep each nudge small so the model doesn't break. That leaves out the whole derivation.\n\nLabs use this on the model itself. It takes thousands of graded attempts and GPUs, which doesn't fit a small budget. Your mission also puts training out of scope.\n\nYour levers are tool descriptions, clear parameter names, and a fixed workflow for steps that never vary. Those fix most bad tool calls at no training cost.\n\nHere's a question to think about. Say the assistant books the wrong machine. Would you look at the tool description first, or at the model's weights, and why?\n\nIf you'd like to go deeper on how models get better at tool calls, I can write a lesson on it.",
